    "created_at": "2020-09-15T09:36:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-09-15T09:36:26Z",
    "body": "I think we might want to define that types which are a wrapper over some other type should by default have conversions to/from that wrapped type, but we might want to define additional conversions and/or override the default ones.\r\n\r\nFor example, let's say that string was defined as a list of code units (which it probably should). Then there'd be a conversion string → list of code units and a conversion list of code units → string by default. But we could additionally define a conversion string ↔ list of code points, and we could in turn use that conversion to define code point length, scalar value string, collect a sequence of code points...\r\n\r\nNow, for some types which add additional semantics to their wrapped types, such as set, we could define an explicit algorithmic conversion list → set which maintains the invariants. And we could use that same thing to handle end-of-queue on I/O queues.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
